      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Optimum tissue levels of magnesium and potassium ions are essential to the structure and function of the myocardium. We hypothesized that an inapparent deficiency of one or both of these elements might contribute to sudden cardiac death in a susceptible person, and in this study investigated levels of these elements in the myocardium of men experiencing sudden death to determine if there was such an association. Subjects dying suddenly with ischemic heart disease had significantly lower levels of myocardial tissue magnesium and potassium than the controls (men dying of acute trauma). The four lowest potassium values were obtained for the only men in the group with a history of angina (P less than 0.0005). Three of the men with angina also had the lowest tissue magnesium levels (P less than 0.005). The intriguing finding gives support to the hypothesis."},
